<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter5_public-and-private-members">
  <title>Public and Private Members</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In the previous section, you saw that a class can provide an
      <term>abstraction</term>: outside code can focus on what an object can do
      without needing to know every detail of how the object stores and manages
      its state.
    </p>

    <p>
      One important tool for creating this abstraction is controlling which
      members are available outside the class.
    </p>

    <p>
      C++ provides access labels such as <c>public:</c> and <c>private:</c>
      for this purpose.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>Public Members</title>

    <p>
      A <term>public member</term> can be accessed by code outside the class.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    double length;
    double width;

    double calculate_area() const;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Because <c>length</c> and <c>width</c> are public, outside code can
      access them directly:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room;

room.length = 12.0;
room.width = 8.0;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The member function is also public, so it can be called from outside the
      class:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double area = room.calculate_area();
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Public members form part of the way other code interacts with an object.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>The Problem with Public Data</title>

    <p>
      Making all data public gives outside code complete freedom to modify the
      object's state.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room;

room.length = -12.0;
room.width = 8.0;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      A negative length may not make sense for the rectangle that our program
      is modeling, but nothing prevents outside code from assigning that value
      when the data member is public.
    </p>

    <p>
      As programs become larger, allowing any part of the program to change an
      object's internal data directly can make it difficult to guarantee that
      the object remains in a sensible state.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Private Members</title>

    <p>
      A <term>private member</term> can be accessed directly by member
      functions of the class, but not by ordinary code outside the class.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
private:
    double length = 0.0;
    double width = 0.0;

public:
    double calculate_area() const;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Now outside code cannot directly write:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room;

room.length = 12.0;   // error: length is private
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      However, a member function of <c>Rectangle</c> can access the private
      members:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double Rectangle::calculate_area() const {
    return length * width;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The data members are hidden from outside code, but they are still
      available to the class's own member functions.
    </p>

    <p>
      Notice that the data members above have initial values:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double length = 0.0;
double width = 0.0;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      These are called default member initializers. They give each newly
      created <c>Rectangle</c> an initial state until we provide a better way
      to initialize objects in the next section.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Providing a Public Interface</title>

    <p>
      If the data is private, outside code needs some public operations for
      working with the object.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, we can provide a member function that changes both
      dimensions only when the new values are valid:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
private:
    double length = 0.0;
    double width = 0.0;

public:
    bool set_dimensions(double new_length, double new_width);
    double calculate_area() const;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The implementation of <c>set_dimensions</c> can check the values before
      changing the object's state:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
bool Rectangle::set_dimensions(double new_length, double new_width) {
    if (new_length <= 0.0 || new_width <= 0.0) {
        return false;
    }

    length = new_length;
    width = new_width;

    return true;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Outside code now asks the object to update itself:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room;

room.set_dimensions(12.0, 8.0);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      rather than changing the internal data directly:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room.length = 12.0;   // not allowed
room.width = 8.0;     // not allowed
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      This gives the class an opportunity to enforce rules whenever its state
      changes.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>The Public Interface and Private Implementation</title>

    <p>
      The public members of a class form its <term>public interface</term>.
      They describe the operations that outside code is allowed to perform on
      objects of that class.
    </p>

    <p>
      The private members are part of the object's internal
      <term>implementation</term>. Outside code does not need direct access to
      those details.
    </p>

    <p>
      For our <c>Rectangle</c> class:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
private:
    double length = 0.0;
    double width = 0.0;

public:
    bool set_dimensions(double new_length, double new_width);
    double calculate_area() const;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      outside code can see and use:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room.set_dimensions(12.0, 8.0);
room.calculate_area();
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      but outside code does not directly manage:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
length
width
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      We can visualize the class as having a public boundary:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
              Rectangle object
     +--------------------------------+
     |       public interface         |
     |                                |
     |  set_dimensions(...)           |
     |  calculate_area()              |
     |                                |
     |  ----------------------------  |
     |       private state            |
     |                                |
     |  length                        |
     |  width                         |
     +--------------------------------+
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Code outside the class works through the public interface rather than
      directly manipulating the private state.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Encapsulation</title>

    <p>
      Combining data with the operations that manage that data, while
      controlling access to the internal state, is called
      <term>encapsulation</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Encapsulation helps a class protect its own state.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, because <c>length</c> and <c>width</c> are private, outside
      code cannot bypass <c>set_dimensions</c> and directly assign negative
      dimensions.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room.set_dimensions(-5.0, 8.0);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The member function can reject that request and leave the object
      unchanged.
    </p>

    <p>
      This also supports abstraction. Code using a <c>Rectangle</c> can focus
      on operations such as:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room.set_dimensions(12.0, 8.0);
double area = room.calculate_area();
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      without needing to manage the object's internal representation directly.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Why Keep Data Private?</title>

    <p>
      Keeping data private provides several useful benefits.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          The class can control how its state changes.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Invalid values can be rejected before they become part of the
          object's state.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Code outside the class depends on the public interface rather than
          on the exact way the data is stored.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The internal implementation can sometimes change without requiring
          every part of the program to change.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      For classes that manage their own state, a good general design guideline
      is to keep data members private unless there is a clear reason to expose
      them directly.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>A Complete Example</title>

    <listing label="chapter5_public_private_AC_1">
      <title>
        This class protects its dimensions and provides a public interface.
      </title>

      <program interactive="activecode"
               line-numbers="yes"
               label="chapter5_public_private_AC_1_editor">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Rectangle {
private:
    double length = 0.0;
    double width = 0.0;

public:
    bool set_dimensions(double new_length, double new_width);
    double calculate_area() const;
};

int main() {
    Rectangle room;

    bool updated = room.set_dimensions(12.0, 8.0);

    if (updated) {
        std::cout << "Area: "
                  << room.calculate_area() << '\n';
    }
    else {
        std::cout << "Invalid dimensions\n";
    }

    return 0;
}

bool Rectangle::set_dimensions(double new_length, double new_width) {
    if (new_length <= 0.0 || new_width <= 0.0) {
        return false;
    }

    length = new_length;
    width = new_width;

    return true;
}

double Rectangle::calculate_area() const {
    return length * width;
}
        ]]></code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      The program can call the public member functions, but it cannot directly
      access <c>length</c> or <c>width</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The class therefore controls how its internal state is changed.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Looking Ahead: Creating Valid Objects</title>

    <p>
      Our <c>Rectangle</c> now protects its private data, but there is still
      something slightly awkward about the way we create and initialize an
      object:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room;

room.set_dimensions(12.0, 8.0);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The object is created first, and then a separate member-function call is
      used to give it the desired dimensions.
    </p>

    <p>
      It would be more convenient if we could create the object with the
      correct starting state in one step.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room(12.0, 8.0);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      C++ provides special member functions called
      <term>constructors</term> for this purpose. We will study constructors
      in the next section.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise label="chapter5_public_private_1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which code can directly access a private data member of a class?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Any function in the program</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Private members are not directly available to ordinary outside
              functions.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>A member function of the class</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Member functions of the class can directly access its private
              members.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Any function that receives the object by reference</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Passing an object by reference does not bypass the class's access
              controls.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_public_private_2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the main purpose of this public function?
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
bool set_dimensions(double new_length, double new_width);
        ]]></program>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              To make the private members public.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The data remains private. The function provides controlled
              access to changing it.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              To provide a controlled way to change the object's state.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A public member function can validate new values before changing
              private data.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              To create a copy of the object.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This member function changes the state of the existing object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_public_private_3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which statement best describes a class's public interface?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The public operations through which outside code uses the
              object.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The public interface defines how other code interacts with the
              object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Every variable declared anywhere in the program.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The public interface consists of the class's publicly accessible
              members.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Only the private data members of the class.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Private data belongs to the internal implementation, not the
              public interface.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_public_private_4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why is keeping a class's data private often useful?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              It prevents member functions from accessing the data.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Member functions can directly access private members.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              It allows the class to control how its internal state is changed.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Controlled access helps protect the object from invalid or
              unintended changes.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              It makes the object unable to change after creation.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Public state-changing member functions can still modify private
              data.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_public_private_5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume <c>room</c> starts with dimensions <c>0.0</c> by
          <c>0.0</c>. What is the final area after these calls?
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
room.set_dimensions(12.0, 8.0);
room.set_dimensions(-5.0, 6.0);
double area = room.calculate_area();
        ]]></program>

        <p>
          Recall that <c>set_dimensions</c> rejects nonpositive dimensions and
          leaves the object unchanged when it rejects them.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>-30.0</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The second call is rejected, so the negative length is never
              stored in the object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>0.0</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The first call succeeds and changes the dimensions before the
              invalid second call is attempted.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>96.0</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The first call stores 12 by 8. The second call is rejected and
              leaves those dimensions unchanged.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_public_private_6"
              number="yes"
              adaptive="yes"
              indentation="hide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Arrange the blocks to define a <c>Room</c> class whose dimensions are
          private and whose public interface lets outside code calculate the
          volume. The member function should not modify the object.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <blocks>
        <block order="1">
          <cline>class Room {</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="2">
          <cline>private:</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="3">
          <cline>    double length = 0.0;</cline>
          <cline>    double width = 0.0;</cline>
          <cline>    double height = 0.0;</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="4">
          <cline>public:</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="5">
          <cline>    double calculate_volume() const;</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="6">
          <cline>};</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>public: double length;</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>    double calculate_volume();</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

</section>
